  "problem": "The ambient temperature in a laboratory varies over time. After $t$ seconds, the temperature (in degrees Celsius) is modeled by\n\n$$\n\u0398(t)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2t+5)+18.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the temperature as a function of $t?$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change is\n{{field-1}} degrees Celsius per second.",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the temperature is ${\u0398}^{\u2032}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u0398}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2t+5)+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2t+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the ambient temperature is $\\frac{2}{2t+5}$ degrees Celsius per second.",
